{
  "$schema": "https://aka.ms/codetour-schema",
  "title": "02 - Next.js basics on your app",
  "description": "How Next.js turns folders into pages, using your own app: layouts, route groups, loading and error screens, API routes.",
  "nextTour": "03 - React basics on your chat page",
  "steps": [
    {
      "directory": "frontend/src/app",
      "title": "Folders become web addresses",
      "description": "**New idea — the App Router:** in Next.js, the folder structure *is* the list of web addresses.\n- A folder with a `page.tsx` becomes a page. `app/(authenticated)/chat/page.tsx` becomes **/chat**.\n- A `layout.tsx` wraps every page in its folder and below, like a picture frame.\n- `loading.tsx` shows while a page loads. `error.tsx` shows if it crashes.\n- A `route.ts` is an API endpoint, not a page.\n\n**Why it matters:** to find the code for any screen, read its URL and follow the folders.\n\n**Watch out:** some folder names have brackets or parentheses. Those are special, and the next steps explain each kind."
    },
    {
      "file": "frontend/src/app/layout.tsx",
      "line": 37,
      "selection": {
        "start": {
          "line": 33,
          "character": 1
        },
        "end": {
          "line": 37,
          "character": 6
        }
      },
      "title": "The root layout",
      "description": "**New idea — layout and children:** `RootLayout` wraps **every** page in the app. `children` is a placeholder for whichever page is being shown.\n\n**How it works:** Next.js calls this function with the current page as `children`. Lines 61–72 put that page inside `<Providers>` and add the `<Toaster>` for pop-up messages.\n\n**Why it matters:** anything placed here appears on every page: fonts, theme, login state.\n\n**Watch out:** `Readonly<{ children: React.ReactNode }>` is TypeScript for *the props can't be changed, and children can be any React content*."
    },
    {
      "file": "frontend/src/app/layout.tsx",
      "line": 31,
      "selection": {
        "start": {
          "line": 21,
          "character": 1
        },
        "end": {
          "line": 31,
          "character": 3
        }
      },
      "title": "Page title and icons",
      "description": "**New idea — metadata:** exporting a `metadata` object tells Next.js what to put in the page's `<head>`: the tab title, the description and the icons.\n\n**How it works:** Next.js reads this when it builds the page. You never write a `<title>` tag by hand.\n\n**Why it matters:** this is how *ADAgentAI* appears in the browser tab and in link previews.\n\n**Watch out:** there are two icons, one for light mode and one for dark mode (`prefers-color-scheme`)."
    },
    {
      "file": "frontend/src/app/layout.tsx",
      "line": 19,
      "selection": {
        "start": {
          "line": 8,
          "character": 1
        },
        "end": {
          "line": 19,
          "character": 4
        }
      },
      "title": "Fonts",
      "description": "**What it is:** two Google fonts: Inter for text, JetBrains Mono for code.\n\n**How it works:** `next/font/google` downloads the fonts when the app is built and serves them from your own site. `variable: \"--font-inter\"` turns each font into a CSS variable, which line 59 attaches to the page.\n\n**Why it matters:** no extra request to Google when a page loads, and no flash of the wrong font.\n\n**Watch out:** the comment mentions Linear. The UI is styled after Linear's look."
    },
    {
      "file": "frontend/src/app/layout.tsx",
      "line": 57,
      "selection": {
        "start": {
          "line": 39,
          "character": 1
        },
        "end": {
          "line": 57,
          "character": 14
        }
      },
      "title": "Hydration and the theme script",
      "description": "**New idea — hydration:** Next.js first sends ready-made HTML, so the page shows quickly. Then React loads in the browser and *takes over* that HTML. That take-over is called hydration.\n\n**How it works:** this small script runs **before** React. It reads the saved theme and adds `dark` or `light` to the page straight away.\n\n**Why it matters:** without it, you'd see a flash of the wrong theme on every load.\n\n**Watch out:** the script changes the `<html>` tag before React arrives, so React would warn that the HTML doesn't match. `suppressHydrationWarning` on line 39 silences that one expected warning."
    },
    {
      "directory": "frontend/src/app/(public)",
      "title": "Route groups: (public) and (authenticated)",
      "description": "**New idea — route groups:** a folder name in parentheses is **left out of the URL**. `app/(public)/pricing/page.tsx` is simply **/pricing**.\n\n**How it works:** a group exists to share a layout. `(public)` holds pages anyone can see: the landing page, login, blog, pricing and terms. `(authenticated)` holds pages that need you to log in: chat, dashboard, providers and settings.\n\n**Why it matters:** each group has its own `layout.tsx`, so logged-in pages get the sidebar and the login check without repeating code.\n\n**Watch out:** the home page is `(public)/page.tsx`, which is **/**."
    },
    {
      "file": "frontend/src/app/(authenticated)/layout.tsx",
      "line": 24,
      "selection": {
        "start": {
          "line": 14,
          "character": 1
        },
        "end": {
          "line": 24,
          "character": 2
        }
      },
      "title": "The logged-in layout",
      "description": "**What it is:** the layout for every logged-in page.\n\n**New idea — Suspense:** `<Suspense fallback={…}>` means *if something inside is still loading, show this instead*. Here that's a spinner.\n\n**How it works:** this file has no `\"use client\"`, so it's a **server** component. It does one thing: it wraps the real work, `AuthenticatedLayoutClient`, in Suspense.\n\n**Why it matters:** splitting it this way keeps the browser-only code in one file, which is the next step."
    },
    {
      "file": "frontend/src/app/(authenticated)/layout-client.tsx",
      "line": 1,
      "selection": {
        "start": {
          "line": 1,
          "character": 1
        },
        "end": {
          "line": 1,
          "character": 13
        }
      },
      "title": "\"use client\"",
      "description": "**New idea — server and client components:** by default, Next.js runs components on the **server** and sends HTML. Writing `\"use client\"` at the top makes a file run in the **browser** too.\n\n**How it works:** you need the browser when a component uses state (`useState`), effects (`useEffect`), clicks, or browser tools like `useRouter` for redirects. This file uses all of those.\n\n**Why it matters:** server components send less JavaScript to the browser. Client components can react to the user. Your app uses both.\n\n**Watch out:** `\"use client\"` also applies to everything this file imports, so keep it as far down the tree as you can."
    },
    {
      "file": "frontend/src/app/(authenticated)/layout-client.tsx",
      "line": 60,
      "selection": {
        "start": {
          "line": 48,
          "character": 1
        },
        "end": {
          "line": 60,
          "character": 94
        }
      },
      "title": "The login check",
      "description": "**What it is:** the code that sends you away if you aren't allowed in.\n\n**How it works:** once your login status has loaded, two checks run:\n- not logged in → go to **/login**\n- logged in but not on the waitlist → go to **/access-denied**\n\nDemo mode skips both.\n\n**Why it matters:** it keeps logged-in pages out of sight for strangers.\n\n**Watch out:** this check runs in the **browser**, so it's a door sign, not a lock. The real lock is the API: every `/api` call checks your token on the server (tour 05). Your app has no Next.js middleware (or proxy) file, so nothing blocks pages on the server side."
    },
    {
      "file": "frontend/src/app/(authenticated)/layout-client.tsx",
      "line": 118,
      "selection": {
        "start": {
          "line": 92,
          "character": 1
        },
        "end": {
          "line": 118,
          "character": 4
        }
      },
      "title": "The app shell",
      "description": "**What it is:** the frame around every logged-in page: the sidebar, the banners and the terms-of-service pop-up.\n\n**How it works:** `{children}` on line 112 is where the current page goes. Everything around it stays in place.\n\n**Why it matters:** layouts **don't reload** when you move between pages. The sidebar keeps its state as you go from Chat to Dashboard.\n\n**Watch out:** the mobile menu button (line 99) only shows on small screens. `md:hidden` is Tailwind for *hide from medium width upwards*."
    },
    {
      "file": "frontend/src/app/(authenticated)/chat/page.tsx",
      "line": 21,
