        "end": {
          "line": 25,
          "character": 2
        }
      },
      "title": "loading.tsx",
      "description": "**New idea — instant loading screens:** if a folder has a `loading.tsx`, Next.js shows it straight away while the page loads.\n\n**How it works:** these grey `Skeleton` blocks copy the real layout: prompt buttons in the middle, the input box at the bottom.\n\n**Why it matters:** the screen never goes blank when you click a link.\n\n**Watch out:** Next.js wraps the page in a Suspense boundary for you here. It's the same idea as the logged-in layout, but automatic."
    },
    {
      "file": "frontend/src/app/error.tsx",
      "line": 17,
      "selection": {
        "start": {
          "line": 1,
          "character": 1
        },
        "end": {
          "line": 17,
          "character": 14
        }
      },
      "title": "error.tsx",
      "description": "**New idea — error boundaries:** if a page crashes while drawing itself, Next.js shows this component instead of a blank screen.\n\n**How it works:** it receives the `error` and a `reset` function. The *Try again* button calls `reset()` to draw the page again.\n\n**Why it matters:** one broken component doesn't take down the whole app.\n\n**Watch out:** error files **must** be client components, hence `\"use client\"`. There's also `global-error.tsx` for crashes in the root layout itself."
    },
    {
      "file": "frontend/src/app/api/[[...route]]/route.ts",
      "line": 10,
      "selection": {
        "start": {
          "line": 1,
          "character": 1
        },
        "end": {
          "line": 10,
          "character": 34
        }
      },
      "title": "API routes and catch-alls",
      "description": "**New idea — route handlers:** a `route.ts` file is an API endpoint, not a page. You export one function per HTTP method: `GET`, `POST`, `PUT`, `DELETE`, `PATCH`.\n\n**New idea — catch-all:** `[...name]` matches one or more parts of a path. With **double** brackets, `[[...route]]` also matches nothing at all. So this file catches `/api` **and** everything under it.\n\n**Why it matters:** this one file sends all `/api` traffic to Hono. That's why the API code doesn't follow Next.js folder rules: Hono has its own router.\n\n**Watch out:** you met this file in tour 01. Now you know why the brackets look like that."
    },
    {
      "file": "frontend/src/app/api/auth/[...path]/route.ts",
      "line": 10,
      "selection": {
        "start": {
          "line": 1,
          "character": 1
        },
        "end": {
          "line": 10,
          "character": 46
        }
      },
      "title": "Neon Auth's routes",
      "description": "**What it is:** the login endpoints, handled by Neon Auth's own code.\n\n**How it works:** `[...path]` (single brackets) catches everything under `/api/auth`. The comment lists what's inside: sign-in, sign-up, sign-out, session, and the callback after Google sign-in.\n\n**Why it matters:** login wasn't written from scratch. Neon Auth provides it, and your code only *checks* the tokens it hands out.\n\n**Watch out:** both this file and `api/[[...route]]` could match `/api/auth/session`. Next.js always picks the **more specific** route, so this one wins."
    },
    {
      "file": "frontend/next.config.ts",
      "line": 30,
      "selection": {
        "start": {
          "line": 24,
          "character": 1
        },
        "end": {
          "line": 30,
          "character": 25
        }
      },
      "title": "Next.js settings",
      "description": "**What it is:** app-wide Next.js settings.\n\n**How it works:**\n- `reactCompiler: true`: React automatically skips re-renders that aren't needed, so hand-written `useMemo` is often unnecessary (your code still has some).\n- `cacheComponents: true`: *Partial Prerendering*. The fixed parts of a page are built ahead of time, and the changing parts stream in.\n\n**Why it matters:** both make pages faster without extra code.\n\n**Watch out:** prerendering runs code at **build** time, where some things break. Your history has the fix, *resolve PPR crypto.randomUUID() error on blog pages* (Feb 9). It's also why the API starts Sentry and analytics lazily, on the first request."
    },
    {
      "title": "What you can do now",
      "description": "### What you can do now\n\n- Find the code for any screen from its URL.\n- Tell a server component (no `\"use client\"`) from a client component.\n- Explain route groups `( )`, dynamic segments `[ ]`, and catch-alls `[...]` and `[[...]]`.\n- Say why the login check in the browser isn't real security, and where the real check is.\n\n**Next:** *03 - React basics on your chat page*. It covers state, effects, props and hooks, using the chat screen."
    }
  ]
}
